> viii | UNEP | Global economic assessment of integrated climate and clean air action
> The economic value of avoided air pollution damage
> exceeds that of avoided climate damage in early
> years after the implementation of the 25 solutions
> and remains nearly 45 per cent of the total economic
> benefit in 2100 (Figure ES1). Considering only the
> costs of climate impacts therefore misses almost half
> of the economic benefits of action, underlining the
> importance of the integrated approach.
> Pursuing climate and air quality action together rather
> than separately generates approximately 0.2 per cent
> of GDP in additional economic benefits. These gains
> arise because many benefit channels – avoided climate
> damage, reduced healthcare expenditure, higher
> labour productivity and quality-of-life improvements
> linked to reduced morbidity and mortality – only
> materialise fully when the climate and clean air
> agendas are pursued together. These benefits are
> captured (but not shown) in Figure ES1.
> The 25 solutions could avoid approximately 0.34 °C
> (0.1–0.6) of warming by 2050 and approximately
> 1.4 °C (0.9–2.1) by 2100 (Figure ES2). Since
> temperatures over land rise faster than the global
> average, the benefits of the 25 solutions are larger at
> the regional level than global figures suggest, with
> avoided warming of approximately 0.1 °C in 2035,
> 0.5 °C in 2050 and 1.5–2 °C in 2100 in most regions.
> Figure ES2: Changes in global surface temperature for the baseline, the four long-term climate solutions
> and the full 25 integrated solutions (integrated action scenario) relative to pre-industrial levels, 2020–2100,
> degrees Celsius.
> Air pollution is a macroeconomic risk,
> not an environmental add-on
> The morbidity and mortality effects of air pollution,
> including through lost productivity and rising
> healthcare costs, already represent several
> percentage points of GDP annually and are
> projected to grow without stronger action.
> This Assessment finds that in 2025, anthropogenic
> ambient (outdoor) fine particulate matter (PM2.5) and
> ozone together caused an estimated 6.4 (5.2–7.3)
> million premature deaths worldwide. Indoor PM2.5
> contributed an additional estimated 2 (1.8–2.2) million
> premature deaths, 300,000 (260,000 to 330,000) of
> which were children. Ambient PM2.5 exposure alone
> is associated annually with millions of new cases of
> childhood asthma, millions of heart attacks, chronic
> obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), diabetes,
> stroke, dementia, and lung cancer (Table ES1).
