> ix | UNEP | Global economic assessment of integrated climate and clean air action
> Cumulatively, by 2050, the 25 integrated solutions
> would avoid hundreds of millions of cases of chronic
> diseases and an estimated 144 million premature
> deaths from ambient and household PM2.5 exposure
> plus ozone exposure. This is made up of an estimated
> 62 (37–77) million cases of childhood asthma, 87
> (65–103) million acute myocardial infarctions (heart
> attacks), 63 (50–73) million cases of COPD, 56 (39–68)
> million cases of diabetes, 45 (37–51) million cases
> of stroke, 36 (29–42) million cases of dementia, 7
> (5.4–8.2) million cases of lung cancer, 96 (80–109)
> million premature deaths attributable to ambient PM2.5,
> 43 (38–48) million premature deaths from household
> PM2.5 exposure, and 5 (4.0–5.8) million premature
> deaths from exposure to ozone (See Figure ES4).
> Many prior economic assessments of air pollution
> have focused on premature mortality, leaving the
> broader costs of illness unvalued. This Assessment
> incorporates the latest evidence across a substantially
> expanded set of health conditions and breaks down
> their cost implications, making the costs and benefits
> of action more visible and directly comparable. By
> 2050, the 25 solutions could avoid market healthcare
> expenditures and productivity losses equal to 0.7 per
> cent GDP. These benefits increase markedly when
> the quality-of-life (non-market) benefits of reduced
> morbidity and mortality are also considered (Figure
> ES3). The avoided market costs are comparable in
> magnitude to the full cost of implementing all 25
> solutions, and the economic case strengthens further
> once non-market welfare losses are included.
> Without further action, emissions of greenhouse gases,
> air pollutants and their precursors are projected to
> keep rising globally, particularly in rapidly developing
> regions. Without additional policy, the share of the
> global population exposed to the most dangerous PM2.5
> concentrations rises from 27 per cent in 2025 to 34
> per cent by 2050. Combined with ageing populations,
> this will increase the disease burden from air pollution
> and raise pressure on public finances and constrain
> economic growth in exactly the countries that can least
> afford it. Full implementation of the 25 solutions would
> avoid 60 to 70 per cent of the total air pollution health
> burden annually.
