> x | UNEP | Global economic assessment of integrated climate and clean air action
> Greater than the sum: integration
> increases returns
> Tackling shared sources of climate change and air
> pollution through an integrated package boosts net
> economic benefits to approximately 11.4 per cent
> of global GDP by 2100, more than either achieves
> alone.
> Because climate change and air pollution share partly
> the same emission sources, the 25 integrated solutions
> can simultaneously reduce greenhouse gases, cut
> air pollution, and generate compounding economic
> returns across health, productivity and avoided climate
> damage (Figure ES4). Integrating the two agendas
> means that in all regions the combined benefits of
> action offset costs in the near term, and substantially
> more so over the long term. Integrated action allows
> impacts to be reduced more quickly, effectively and at
> a lower overall cost compared to the situation pursuing
> each of the agendas separately with uncoordinated
> timelines and stakeholder involvement hampering fast
> progress and transformation needed to implement it.
> By 2050 the 25 solutions cut global carbon dioxide
> emissions by half, methane by 60 per cent, and black
> carbon and PM2.5 precursors including sulphur dioxide
> and nitrogen oxides by 70 per cent (Figure ES4). By
> 2100 carbon dioxide emissions are net-negative and
> PM2.5 precursors fall by up to 85 per cent. Solutions
> targeting air pollution and near-term climate impacts
> complement long-term decarbonization by delivering
> immediate economic gains, including reductions
> in public healthcare costs, productivity gains, and
> increased workforce participation.
> Figure ES3: Annual morbidity and mortality air-pollution-related costs avoided by implementing all 25
> solutions, 2025–2100, per cent of global gross domestic product.
