> xii | UNEP | Global economic assessment of integrated climate and clean air action
> The benefits materialise everywhere,
> across all regions and over different
> timescales
> The economic and health returns from integrated
> action vary substantially across the 15 regions
> analysed, but every region benefits, and no solution
> is wasted.
> The 25 integrated solutions do not deliver uniform
> benefits across the world. Benefit-cost ratios (BCRs)
> range from three in high-income regions such as the
> European Union, the European Free Trade Association
> and the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern
> Ireland to 26 in Southern Africa, reflecting the
> concentration of health and climate risk in lowerincome
> countries in which approximately 95 per cent
> of premature deaths attributable to air pollution occur.
> Regions in South Asia, Sub-Saharan Africa and the
> Middle East face combined pollution and climate risks
> equivalent to more than 5 per cent of regional GDP by
> 2100. Even excluding non-market benefits entirely,
> benefits exceed costs in 8 of the 15 regions. No region
> should wait for others to move first.
> Within the 15 regions analysed, the solutions delivering
> the largest near-term benefits differ substantially,
> reflecting each region’s energy system, pollution profile
> and stage of development (Figure ES6). In 2035, five
> broad groupings emerge where countries gain the most
> benefit: for high-income regions with mature pollution
> controls it is from dietary change and emissions
> controls for international shipping; for developing
> regions with high indoor air pollution exposure,
> including Latin America and the Caribbean, South Asia
> and Sub-Saharan Africa, it is from clean cooking; for oil
> and gas producing regions in the Middle East, North
> Africa and Russian Federation it is from controlling
> methane leakage and flaring; and for rapidly developing
> regions across Asia it is from power generation and
> household energy transitions.
> These groupings remain broadly stable in 2050, but
> the relative importance of the four long-term climate
> solutions grows as near-term air pollution benefits are
> progressively captured, and dietary change and energyefficiency
> standards rise in prominence across most
> regions. This variation in the benefits that the measures
> give does not reduce the case for implementing all
