> xv | UNEP | Global economic assessment of integrated climate and clean air action
> Enabling conditions are as important
> as technology choices
> Access to finance, institutional capacity,
> governance, and coordination challenges explain
> why the same policies deliver different outcomes
> across contexts. Investment in a well-designed
> enabling environment is essential to bringing full
> implementation forward by up to a decade.
> Most previous economic assessments have focused
> on which solutions need to be adopted by when, with
> limited attention on what makes implementation
> succeed or fail over different time periods. This
> Assessment addresses that gap directly. It also shows
> that barriers to implementation are not primarily
> technical. Siloed decision-making, limited enforcement
> capacity, inadequate financing and weak crossministerial
> coordination are the constraints that are
> cited most frequently to explain why proven solutions
> are not adopted at the speed or scale predicted in
> models and fail to achieve their technical potential
> (Figure ES7).
> Addressing these barriers through a suite of broader
> cross-cutting institutional enablers and narrowersector
> specific reforms can accelerate implementation
> substantially (Figure ES8). The 25 solutions span
> six sectors, each governed by different ministries,
> regulatory frameworks and financing channels.
> Realizing the full package of benefits requires
> institutional architecture capable of coordinating
> across all of them – and this Assessment shows, for
> the first time, the economic value of strengthening
> institutional coordination and equipping regulators
> with the tools and resources to enforce policies.
> Figure ES7: The relative importance of different barriers, years delayed.
> Note: The error bars reflect upper and lower ranges of 95 per cent confidence intervals for all solutions for each
> type of barrier for the 4 countries, China, India, Mexico and Nigeria.
