> Figure ES9: Comparison of the key greenhouse gas emissions reduction between the integrated scenario
> and the Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change overshoot pathways, 2010–2100, methane, black carbon
> and nitrous oxide, million tonnes; carbon dioxide, gigatonnes.
> Full adoption exceeds the pace required to meet
> the World Health Organization (WHO) 2040 airquality
> mortality target and is consistent with
> Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change (IPCC)
> pathways for limiting peak warming.
> In May 2025, the World Health Assembly adopted a
> voluntary target of halving mortality attributable to
> anthropogenic air pollution by 2040, relative to 2015.
> Meeting it requires average annual reductions in
> attributable mortality of approximately 3.3 per cent.
> Global implementation of the 25 integrated solutions
> delivers approximately 4 per cent per year over the
> same period, exceeding the pace the target demands.
> In the 2015 Paris Agreement, parties agreed to limit
> global warming to well below 2 °C and ideally to
> 1.5 °C. Exceeding the 1.5 °C climate target of the Paris
> Agreement is now widely assessed as unavoidable; the
> central challenge is to limit peak warming, reduce the
> duration of exceedance, and return temperatures below
> 1.5 °C. For the super pollutants that determine the peak
> and pace of overshoot – methane, tropospheric ozone,
> HFCs and black carbon – the 25 integrated solutions
> reduce associated emissions to levels consistent
> with, or well below, IPCC overshoot, peak and decline
> pathways limiting warming to 1.5 °C (Figure ES9).
> For carbon dioxide, the solutions achieve substantial
> reductions, reaching net-negative emissions after
> 2080, but closing the remaining gap to the net-zero by
> 2060 required by these pathways will require additional
> decarbonization, land-use change and carbon capture
> measures that do not meet the criteria for inclusion in
> the package of 25 solutions. In an era of overshoot,
> the 25 solutions deliver across all three stages of
> the response: rapid near-term reductions that slow
> warming now, sustained decarbonization that limits
> the peak, and the net-negative carbon dioxide and
> continued super pollutant reductions that help bring
> temperatures back down while delivering immediate
> benefits for public health and the economy.
> xviii | UNEP | Global economic assessment of integrated climate and clean air action
> Call to action
> This Assessment makes a more comprehensive
> economic case for integrated action on climate and
> clean air than any previous global analysis. Capturing
> these returns, at speed and scale, requires deliberate
> choices across five areas.
> • Make integrated climate and clean
