> **Blog Post Title:**  
> The Economic and Health Case for Integrated Climate and Clean Air Action
> 
> **Blog Post:**  
> 
> Climate change and air pollution are often treated as separate policy problems. But they share many sources—and solutions. Action to cut fossil-fuel emissions, methane leaks, dirty cooking fuels, and transport pollution can reduce warming while delivering immediate gains for health, productivity, and public finances.
> 
> I contributed to Chapter 3 of UNEP’s new *Global Economic Assessment of Integrated Climate and Clean Air Action*, which assesses the combined climate and pollution risks and the economic value of addressing them together.
> 
> The assessment finds that a package of 25 integrated solutions can generate benefits that exceed implementation costs rapidly, while avoiding major climate damages and a vast burden of air-pollution-related disease. By 2050, the measures could avoid an estimated 144 million premature deaths cumulatively, alongside large reductions in chronic illness, healthcare costs, and lost productivity.
> 
> Crucially, delay is costly: near-term emissions reductions and health benefits cannot simply be recovered later. Integrated action is therefore not an environmental add-on, but a core economic and public-health strategy.
> 
